The '''Kingdom of Guajicutea''' (1536–1609) was a vassal state of the [[Burawa|Burawi]] colonial empire, established on the island of [[Guajicutea]] in 1536 and dissolved by [[Sarta|Sartan]] colonization in 1609. The kingdom replaced the traditional [[Cacique Má]] governance system — an elected king validated by a high priestess — with a hereditary Christian monarchy under Burawi suzerainty. It was ruled by four [[Boqor|Boqors]] over its 73-year existence, ending with the deposition of [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Carlos I]] during the [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] (1608–1609).

The kingdom's history was shaped by the [[Great Pestilence]] (1531), which killed approximately 30 percent of the population; the introduction of chattel slavery (1543) and its abolition (1571); the [[First Guaji Civil War]] (1554); the rivalry between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]] over influence in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}}; and the gradual loss of sovereignty to Sarta, culminating in colonial annexation.

== Etymology ==

The kingdom was named for the island of [[Guajicutea]]. The title '''Boqor''' was a [[Burawa|Burawi]] royal title, adopted by the kingdom's rulers in place of the traditional Guaji title '''[[Cacique Má]]''' (elected king). The use of a foreign royal title symbolized the kingdom's subordination to Burawi authority and the replacement of the indigenous electoral system with hereditary rule.

== History ==

=== Establishment (1536) ===

The kingdom was established in 1536 by Governor [[Yohannes Tadesse]] of the [[Lacashe]] colony, following the death of [[Yabí Tabá]], the last [[Cacique Má]]. Yabí Tabá had sent his eldest son to Lacashe in 1523 to study among the Burawi as part of a succession arrangement with Governor [[Dawit Wolde]]. The son returned in 1536 baptized and bearing the Burawi royal title of Boqor, bypassing the traditional electoral system that had governed Guajicutea for a millennium.

The new king, crowned [[Petros I of Guajicutea|Boqor Petros I]], was a Christian of house [[Venturan (feudal territory)|Venturan]]. His accession triggered the [[Warí Buka]] (1536), a civil war in which the religious class — boíques and priestesses — and peasants rose against the foreign-backed claimant. The revolt was crushed by Burawi military force, establishing the kingdom on a foundation of external support.

=== Great Pestilence and early crisis (1531–1547) ===

The [[Great Pestilence]] — primarily {{w|smallpox}} — struck Guajicutea in February 1531, before the kingdom's formal establishment. The mass death wave killed approximately 12,000 of a population of approximately 40,000, reducing the island to approximately 25,000–28,000. Trade stopped during the crisis, fields were fallowed, and famine lasted until 1538. Guaji cremation practices, in use since 516 CE, reduced disease spread compared to other Capuyaquiran peoples.

The [[Bari Buka]] (1533) was the first rebellion after the disease subsided, crushed with Burawi military support. The [[Dabra]] mission, established in 1532, served as both a religious and diplomatic outpost of the Burawi Catholic Church. The [[Emissary of Burawa]] represented Burawi political interests from a fort near the port of [[Waribá]].

Burawi military presence consisted of three captains and three battalions — two in Waribá and one in [[Tabakurí]]. The posting was considered a vacation by officers compared to the New Burawa front lines against the [[Aruanãyára]] Empire. The 1547 census recorded approximately 875 Burawi personnel — roughly 3.4 percent of the Guaji population of 25,000.

=== Chattel slavery and plantation economy (1543–1571) ===

Chattel slavery was introduced in October 1543, when the first enslaved [[Kaftians|Kaftian]] was sold at Waribá to the bikabári of [[Ganan (feudal territory)|Ganan]]. Kaftians were brought from the continent of [[Kaftia]] by the Burawi slave trade, which had begun in 1533. Enslaved Kaftians were put to work on plantation estates in southern Guajicutea, which became the center of cash crop production for export to Burawi colonial markets.

The southern bikabás became the wealthiest territorial lords due to plantation exports. The [[Bukan (feudal territory)|Bukan]] bikabá controlled access to gold mines in the northern highlands, leading to the [[Bukan-Wakunan conflict|Bukan-Wakunan gold war]], mediated by Burawi forces whose primary interest was gold revenue.

The [[Guaji Peasant Rebellion]] of 1571 — in which peasants actively revolted against plantation estates and southern bikabás — led to a ban on slave imports. The economic depression of 1573 ended the slave economy, and approximately 800 Kaftians were assimilated into Guaji society as free people.

=== First Guaji Civil War (1554) ===

{{main|First Guaji Civil War}}

The [[First Guaji Civil War]] (1554) was a four-month conflict triggered by the death of Petros I on January 14, 1554. His first son, [[Petro II of Guajicutea|Petro II]], was crowned and assassinated at his coronation on the same day, February 3, 1554. The civil war pitted south and west factions against east and Waribá loyalists. [[Volisania]] sent weapons and impressed Guaji ships into service. [[Burawa]] sent a fleet but recalled it after one month.

The war ended with the installation of Petros I's second son, [[Dawit I of Guajicutea|Dawit I]], as a Volisania-backed ruler. The post-war settlement gave Volisania all islands west of Guajicutea, established the [[Petran Catholic]] Church in Tabakurí, and permitted the construction of a Volisanian fort at [[Barikú]]. Burawi military was reduced to a minor battalion stationed in Waribá. The conspirator [[Buka Wariban]], Lord of Waribá, seized the Dabra mission during the war and imprisoned its religious staff.

=== Relative peace and colonial withdrawal (1554–1590) ===

Dawit I reigned for 44 years (1554–1598), the longest reign of any Boqor. The period was marked by relative peace, population recovery from approximately 26,000 to approximately 34,500, and the operation of the plantation economy with enslaved Kaftian labor until the rebellion of 1571 and the subsequent end of slavery.

The [[Kingdom of Murani]] was established in 1564 when Dawit I arranged a Burawi protectorate for the Murani islands (Maruba, Dukira, Waturi). The [[Caracuran (feudal territory)|Caracuran]] bikabári married daughters to the Murani king, linking the two peoples through the Caracurí maritime tradition. The [[Costabella Agreement]] (1565) coordinated Burawi and Volisanian conquests in the Capuyaquiran.

Southern gold mining began in 1570 along [[Monte Yukumá]] rivers, breaking a thousand-year taboo against mining the sacred volcano. The [[Third Capuyaquiran War]] (1578–1581) saw Burawa annex the remaining Greater Capuyaquiran islands, excluding the Guaji and Murani protectorates. By 1590, only Guajicutea and Murani remained independent, as protectorates; all other islands had been annexed by Burawa or Volisania.

In the 1580s, as colonial demand for Guaji cash crops declined and the plantation economy collapsed, Dawit I courted [[Mercia]], [[Breisland]], and [[Sarta]] as new trade partners to restore exports.

=== War of the Mesogean and loss of sovereignty (1590–1609) ===

{{main|History of Guajicutea (1590-1609)}}

The [[War of the Mesogean]] (1590–1610) between Burawa and Volisania was fought primarily in the Old World. In the New World, the conflict involved naval impressment and ship captures, but no land fighting on Guajicutea. Both powers feared giving [[Sarta]], [[Mercia]], or [[Breisland]] a pretext for intervention.

Dawit I died in 1598 after 44 years on the throne. His son [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Carlos I]] — the first king with a Sartan given name — acceded. A Sartan emissary arrived in 1600. The hurricane of 1601 devastated the island, and Sarta provided substantial aid, creating a dependency relationship. The Sarta-Guajicutea formal alliance of 1605 angered Burawa, which was at war. Sarta purchased Burawa's Waribá fort for 50,000 gold coins in 1607, removing the last Burawi military presence. The Sarta-Volisania neutrality deal of 1608 led Volisania to withdraw.

The [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] (1608–1609) ended the kingdom. Sarta sent a fleet, captured [[Tabakurí]], and deposed Carlos I. The [[Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]] was established in 1609, ending the Boqor line and approximately 1,000 years of Guaji self-governance.

== Government ==

The kingdom was a hereditary monarchy under Burawi suzerainty. The king bore the title '''[[Boqor]]''', a Burawi royal title that replaced the indigenous '''[[Cacique Má]]''' (elected king). Unlike the Cacique Má, who was elected by village caciques and confirmed by the [[Warí]] (high priestess), the Boqor inherited the throne and was validated by Burawi colonial authority rather than Guaji religious tradition.

=== Feudal system ===

The kingdom's territory was divided into sixteen '''bikabás''' (marked lands), each governed by a '''bikabári''' (lord of the marked land). The bikabári replaced the pre-colonial caciques as territorial rulers, converting loyal chiefs into hereditary feudal vassals with Christianized family names. The feudal family names followed a pattern of Guaji lineage or place name plus the suffix '''-an''', an East African Christian adaptation: Guanaran, Barikan, Karuban, Wariban, Taban, Yakuban, Makuan, Yukuman, Bukan, Kurun, Wakunan, Caracuran, Venturan, Trogonan, Barian, and Ganan.

The bikabás reported to the Boqor and, through him, to the Burawi colonial administration. The southern bikabás, controlling plantation agriculture, were the wealthiest; the Bukan bikabá controlled gold mine access in the northern highlands.

=== Religious reforms ===

The establishment of the kingdom brought sweeping religious reforms. The traditional religious order — boíques and priestesses — was abolished. The [[Catacumbas del Abismo|catacombs]] were sealed. The native lunar calendar was replaced by the Burawi solar calendar. Political institutions were formalized under Burawi administration. The priestess class, which had held ultimate authority in disputes under the old system, was stripped of political power.

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] was the official religion. The three powers of Guaji cosmology — sea, mountain, and sky — were mapped onto the {{w|Trinity|Holy Trinity}}: sea as God the Father (creator), mountain as God the Son (judgment and fertility), sky as the Holy Spirit (spirits and messengers). This syncretic mapping began the development of Guaji Catholicism, which absorbed indigenous spiritual elements into the Catholic framework even as the official church hierarchy resisted popular practices.

Cremation, practiced since 516 CE, remained a point of tension. The church pressed for Christian burial; Guaji communities maintained cremation where possible. A hybrid practice emerged in some communities: a Catholic funeral rite followed by cremation rather than interment.

=== Burawi administration ===

The [[Emissary of Burawa]] served as the Burawi political representative, based in a fort near the port of Waribá. The [[Dabra]] mission, established in 1532, served as both a religious outpost and diplomatic station, led by a '''[[Liqa]]''' (head priest). The Burawi military presence — three battalions in peacetime — was a show of force that was usually idle. The Emissary drew workers from Burawa and New Burawa, not from local labor.

== Territory ==

The kingdom's territory was the island of Guajicutea, approximately 3,278 km², the largest island in the Greater Capuyaquiran. The kingdom did not control the surrounding archipelago, though pre-colonial Guajicutea had maintained a tributary system over eleven eastern islands (c. 1290–1360 CE). Under Burawi colonial rule, these islands were administered separately by the colonial government, not by the kingdom.

The capital was [[Tabakurí]], an inland city at approximately 200 meters elevation in central-south Guajicutea, built after the great hurricane of 1143 CE. The kingdom inherited Tabakurí from the pre-colonial period. The primary ports were [[Waribá]] (northeast, the main trade and military port) and [[Yukubá]] (southwest, the ancient western port). [[Barikú]] (west) served as a secondary port and, after 1554, as the site of the Volisanian fort.

== Economy ==

The kingdom's economy was dominated by plantation agriculture in the southern lowlands, producing cash crops for export to Burawi colonial markets. Enslaved [[Kaftians|Kaftian]] labor was the primary labor source from 1543 until the slave import ban of 1571 and the end of slavery in 1573.

Gold mining in the northern highlands was controlled by the Bukan bikabá. Gold had been worked from stream gravels and weathered veins since pre-colonial times, worked cold with no smelting. Southern gold mining along Monte Yukumá rivers began in 1570, breaking a thousand-year taboo against mining the sacred volcano.

Trade flowed through Waribá, which served as the colony's primary port and as a transit hub between the Volisanian colony of [[Balboa]] (Costabella) and the Burawi colony of Lacashe (Guledga). The transit route ran Costabella → Waribá (~1,100 km) → Guledga (~614 km).

After the end of slavery in 1573 and the collapse of the plantation economy, the kingdom's economy diversified. Dawit I's courtship of Mercia, Breisland, and Sarta in the 1580s sought to restore cash crop exports through new trade partners as colonial demand declined.

== Demographics ==

The Great Pestilence (1531) reduced the population from approximately 40,000 to approximately 25,000–28,000. The population recovered slowly: approximately 26,000 in 1555, approximately 34,500 by 1590. The 1547 census recorded approximately 875 Burawi colonial personnel — roughly 3.4 percent of the Guaji population.

The kingdom's population was predominantly Guaji, with small communities of Burawi colonial personnel (military, mission, political, and merchant families). After 1543, enslaved Kaftians were brought to work the plantations. After the end of slavery in 1573, approximately 800 Kaftians were assimilated as free people. A small Bayani community had been present since the early colonial period.

== Military ==

The kingdom relied on Burawi military forces for internal security and external defense. Three Burawi battalions were stationed on the island — two at Waribá and one at Tabakurí — though the posting was considered a peacetime garrison. In 1553, two of three battalions were withdrawn to support the Burawi war against the [[Aruanãyára]] Empire, leaving a skeleton force. After the First Guaji Civil War (1554), Burawi military was further reduced to a minor battalion at Waribá.

Guaji warriors were hired as mercenaries by both Burawa and Volisania to serve as enforcers in colonial subjugation of other Capuyaquiran peoples. Guaji knowledge of Capuyaquiran languages and customs made them effective intermediaries.

